    "note": "ITU Region 2 (North, Central and South America, the Caribbean, Greenland and the eastern Pacific). Every range here was checked against the Region 2 column of the International Table of Frequency Allocations as reproduced in the FCC Online Table of Frequency Allocations (47 CFR 2.106, https://transition.fcc.gov/oet/spectrum/table/fcctable.pdf, which restates ITU Radio Regulations Article 5), against the machine-readable Canadian Table of Frequency Allocations published by ISED (https://ised-isde.canada.ca/sat-oas/en/lists/ctfa.csv, Canada being a Region 2 administration), and for the amateur bands against the Region 2 column of 47 CFR 97.301 (eCFR). Aeronautical assignments were checked against 47 CFR 87.173 and broadcast band edges against 47 CFR 73.14, 73.201 and 73.603. Region 2 differs from Region 1 in ways this file reflects deliberately: mediumwave runs 535-1705 kHz (ITU footnotes 5.86 and 5.89) rather than 526.5-1606.5 kHz, and is channelised on a 10 kHz raster with carriers from 540 to 1700 kHz (47 CFR 73.14) rather than 9 kHz; 160 m is 1800-2000 kHz rather than 1810-1850; 80/75 m runs to 4000 kHz; 40 m is 7000-7300 kHz with broadcasting starting at 7300 kHz rather than 7200; the tropical broadcast bands (120 m, 90 m, 60 m and the 75 m broadcast band) are NOT allocated in Region 2; and Region 2 has the 1.25 m amateur band at 220-225 MHz, which Region 1 does not. The two marine VHF blocks are drawn to the OUTER EDGES of the outermost ITU Appendix 18 channels (each channel is 25 kHz wide, so the block runs from the lowest channel centre minus 12.5 kHz to the highest plus 12.5 kHz); that is why the ship block starts at 156.0125 rather than at channel 60's centre of 156.025, and it is what lets the distress and AIS channels nest inside their blocks. INFORMATIONAL ONLY - a display aid, not an authoritative or licensing reference. Intentionally coarse: national variations within the region (which are substantial above 30 MHz) are left to the per-country plans, and channel plans are not modelled. JSON has no comment syntax, so this note lives in the data; the loader ignores unknown keys.",
